1. Introduction
1.1 Purpose
This document specifies what the Circular Campus system must do, version 1.0, for the COSC 336 prototype. It turns the Phase 1 findings and the Phase 2 feasibility decision (GO, with scope control) into numbered, testable requirements, use cases and a use-case diagram. It says what the system must do, not how it will be built; architecture and design follow in Phases 4–5.
1.2 Product Scope
The system is an internal web application where Khalifa University departments register assets, publish surplus items, request resources, approve and record transfers, and track each asset through maintenance and end of life. Five AI features support users: asset classification, semantic matching and ranking, sustainable-action recommendations, an LLM assistant, and generated reports. AI output is always advice; an authorised person makes every important decision. Public access, live links to existing university systems and demand forecasting are out of scope for this release.
1.3 Document Conventions
•	IDs. Functional requirements are FR-<feature>-<n> (e.g. FR-REQ-3). Non-functional requirements are NFR-<n>, business rules BR-<n>, use cases UC-<n>.
•	Wording. "Shall" marks a mandatory requirement. Requirements follow the EARS patterns from the lecture (ubiquitous, "When…", "While…", "If… then…").
•	Priority. Every requirement has its own MoSCoW priority: M = Must (essential prototype function), S = Should (desirable extension), C = Could (future enhancement if time allows). Priorities are not inherited.
•	TBD. Values still to be agreed with stakeholders are marked [TBD] and listed in Section 8.
1.4 Intended Audience
The lab instructor reviews the full document. Developers should read Sections 2–5; testers should use the numbered requirements and the use-case flows to write Phase 7 test cases. Stakeholders (department representatives, custodians) can read Sections 2 and 6 to confirm the workflows.
