6.2 Use-Case Diagrams and Descriptions
UC-1: Log In
 
UC-1 use-case diagram
Description: A user proves their identity and gets the screens for their role.
Actors: Campus User (primary).
Preconditions: User has an active account.
Trigger: User opens the system.
Main flow:
1.	User enters university email and password.
2.	System verifies the credentials.
3.	System loads the user's roles and department.
4.	System opens the dashboard showing only functions allowed for those roles (FR-ACC-3).
Alternative flows: 4a. Session idle for 30 minutes: system logs the user out and returns to step 1 (FR-ACC-6).
Exception flows: 2a. Wrong password: system shows an error. 2b. Fifth wrong attempt in a row: system locks the account for 15 minutes (FR-ACC-4). 2c. Account deactivated: system refuses login and tells the user to contact the administrator.
Postconditions: User session started; login recorded in the audit log.
UC-2: Register Asset
 
UC-2 use-case diagram
Description: An authorised user records a new asset so it appears in the shared inventory with a standard category.
Actors: Department Representative or Asset Custodian (primary, initiator); AI Service (secondary).
Preconditions: User is logged in (UC-1) and has register permission for the department.
Trigger: User clicks "Add Asset".
Main flow:
1.	User enters name, description, quantity, location, purchase date, estimated value and condition, and attaches photos.
2.	System sends the name and description to the AI Service (UC-3).
3.	System shows the suggested category, tags and standard description with a confidence value.
4.	User accepts or edits the suggestions.
5.	System validates all required fields.
6.	System saves the asset with a unique asset ID and status Registered.
7.	System records a "Registered" event in the asset history and confirms to the user.
Alternative flows:
•	3a. AI Service unavailable: system shows the normal category list; user picks a category manually; flow continues at step 5.
•	3b. Confidence below 0.6: suggestion is shown with a "Low confidence" label.
Exception flows:
•	5a. Required field missing or invalid (e.g. negative value, file over 5 MB): system highlights the field and does not save.
Postconditions: Asset stored with status Registered; history and audit entries created; AI suggestion and user decision logged.
Rules: BR-1 (one department, one custodian).
UC-3: Classify Asset with AI
 
UC-3 use-case diagram
Description: Included in UC-2; the AI suggests how to describe and categorise a new asset.
Actors: AI Service (secondary); started by UC-2.
Preconditions: Name and description entered in UC-2.
Trigger: UC-2 step 2.
Main flow:
1.	System sends the name, description and specifications to the AI Service.
2.	AI Service returns category, subcategory, up to 8 tags, material type, standard description and confidence.
3.	System lists any missing fields as prompts (FR-AI-3).
4.	System returns the suggestion to UC-2 for the user to accept or edit (FR-AI-2, FR-AI-18).
Alternative flows: 2a. Confidence below 0.6: suggestion marked "Low confidence".
Exception flows: 1a. AI Service unavailable: UC-2 continues with manual category selection.
Postconditions: Suggestion, model version and user decision logged.
UC-4: Publish Surplus Asset
 
UC-4 use-case diagram
Description: A department makes an unused asset visible in the campus marketplace.
Actors: Department Representative or authorised Custodian (primary).
Preconditions: Asset exists, belongs to the user's department and is Registered, In Use or back from maintenance.
Trigger: User clicks "Publish as Surplus" on the asset page.
Main flow:
1.	System shows the asset details and asks for an availability note and optional end date.
2.	User confirms the condition and enters the note.
3.	System sets the status to Available.
4.	System adds the listing to the marketplace.
5.	System checks open requests and notifies requesters with a match score of 0.75 or more (FR-NOT-3).
Alternative flows: 2a. Condition has changed: user updates it before publishing.
Exception flows: 1a. Asset is Under Maintenance or Pending Disposition: system blocks publishing and shows the reason.
Postconditions: Asset is Available and visible to all logged-in users; event logged.
UC-5: Search Assets
 
UC-5 use-case diagram
Description: A user finds available assets before buying new ones.
Actors: Any Campus User (primary).
Preconditions: User is logged in.
Trigger: User types a keyword or opens the marketplace.
Main flow:
1.	User enters a keyword and optional filters (category, condition, department, location, availability, age, value).
2.	System returns matching assets the user is allowed to see, sorted by relevance.
3.	User opens an asset to see photos, condition, location and history summary.
4.	User may continue to UC-6 to request it.
Alternative flows: 2a. User changes the sort order or filters; system refreshes the list.
Exception flows: 2b. No results: system shows "No matching assets" and offers to submit a general request (UC-6).
Postconditions: None stored (search is read-only).
UC-6: Submit Resource Request
 
UC-6 use-case diagram
Description: A requester asks for a resource, either a specific listing or a described need that the AI matches.
Actors: Requester (primary, initiator); Department Representative of the owning department (secondary, receives the request); AI Service (secondary, via UC-7).
Preconditions: Requester is logged in and has request permission.
Trigger: Requester clicks "Request" on a listing, or "New Request".
Main flow:
1.	Requester enters category, purpose, specifications, quantity, preferred condition, urgency, location and required date.
2.	System filters available assets by hard rules and asks the AI Service to score them (UC-7).
3.	System shows the top 5 matches with scores and reasons.
4.	Requester selects one asset.
5.	System saves the request as Submitted and reserves the asset for 3 working days.
6.	System notifies the owning department's representative.
7.	Requester sees the request in "My Requests" with its status.
Alternative flows:
•	1a. Requester started from a listing: the asset is pre-selected; flow goes to step 5.
•	3a. No suitable match: requester saves the request as Open; system notifies them when a matching listing appears.
•	5a. Asset already reserved: request joins the queue for that asset.
Exception flows:
•	2a. AI Service fails or takes over 10 s: system shows rule-based keyword results instead and says AI ranking is unavailable.
•	5b. Required field missing: system highlights it and does not save.
Postconditions: Request stored as Submitted (or Open); asset Reserved; notification sent; AI match list logged.
Rules: BR-7 (3-day reservation limit).
UC-7: Match Assets with AI
 
UC-7 use-case diagram
Description: Included in UC-6; ranks available assets against a request.
Actors: AI Service (secondary); started by UC-6.
Preconditions: Request details entered in UC-6.
Trigger: UC-6 step 2.
Main flow:
1.	System removes assets that fail hard rules (not Available, quantity too low, condition below minimum).
2.	System sends the request and remaining assets to the AI Service for semantic scoring.
3.	System combines the score with location, urgency, date, transfer and repair cost into a 0–1 compatibility score.
4.	System ranks equal-suitability matches higher if they give more waste diversion or carbon reduction.
5.	System returns the top 5, each with a plain-language reason.
Alternative flows: 1a. No asset passes the hard rules: system returns an empty list and UC-6 continues at 3a.
Exception flows: 2a. AI Service fails or takes over 10 s: rule-based keyword search is used instead (FR-AI-9).
Postconditions: Match list, scores and model version logged; the requester's choice or override recorded (FR-AI-18).
UC-8: Review Request
 
UC-8 use-case diagram
Description: The owning department approves or rejects a request for its asset.
Actors: Department Representative (primary); Requester and Notification Service (secondary).
Preconditions: A Submitted request exists for an asset owned by the representative's department.
Trigger: Representative opens the request from a notification or the approvals list.
Main flow:
1.	System shows the request, the asset, the AI match score and reason.
2.	Representative reviews and clicks Approve.
3.	System sets the request to Approved and creates a Scheduled transfer (UC-9).
4.	System notifies the requester.
Alternative flows: 2a. Representative rejects with a reason: system sets Rejected, releases the reservation, notifies the requester and suggests up to 3 alternatives.
Exception flows:
•	2b. Representative is also the requester: system blocks the approval (BR-3) and routes it to an administrator.
•	2c. Reservation expired before review: system marks the request Expired and informs both sides. 2d. Asset value above the set limit: system also requires administrator approval (FR-APR-6).
Postconditions: Request is Approved or Rejected; decision, approver and time logged.
UC-9: Record Transfer
↗ UC-9 use-case diagram
Description: The move of an approved asset is recorded until the receiver confirms it.
Actors: Department Representative of sending department (primary); Asset Custodian of receiving department (secondary).
Preconditions: Request is Approved; transfer is Scheduled.
Trigger: Sending side clicks "Mark Dispatched".
Main flow:
1.	Sender confirms collection or delivery date and attaches a handover form.
2.	System sets the transfer to Dispatched and the asset to In Transfer, and notifies the receiver.
3.	Receiving custodian confirms receipt and the condition on arrival.
4.	System updates the asset's department, custodian, location and status (In Use).
5.	System sets the request to Fulfilled and asks whether it avoided a purchase (FR-SUS-1).
Alternative flows: 1a. Date changes: sender edits the schedule; receiver is notified.
Exception flows: 3a. Asset damaged or missing on arrival: receiver reports it; transfer becomes Disputed; both departments and the administrator are notified.
Postconditions: Ownership and location updated; transfer Completed; history shows the move.
Rules: BR-2 (no department change without approval and confirmed receipt).
UC-10: Record Maintenance
↗ UC-10 use-case diagram
Description: Maintenance staff log an inspection or repair and update the asset's condition.
Actors: Maintenance Staff (primary); Asset Custodian (secondary, reports the defect).
Preconditions: A defect has been reported on the asset (status Under Maintenance).
Trigger: Maintenance staff open the job from their queue.
Main flow:
1.	Staff record the inspection result.
2.	Staff record repair actions, parts, cost (AED) and date.
3.	Staff mark the job Resolved and set the new condition.
4.	System returns the asset to its previous status and notifies the custodian.
Alternative flows: 1a. No fault found: staff close the job; asset returns to its previous status.
Exception flows: 1b. Asset not repairable: staff mark it Not Repairable; system runs UC-12 («extend») and notifies the department representative.
Postconditions: Maintenance record and cost saved; condition updated; history entry added.
UC-11: Process End-of-Life Action
↗ UC-11 use-case diagram
Description: A department proposes donation, disassembly, recycling or disposal; a sustainability officer decides.
Actors: Department Representative (primary, initiator); Sustainability Officer (secondary, approver); AI Service (via UC-12).
Preconditions: Asset belongs to the representative's department and is not in a final state.
Trigger: Representative clicks "Propose End-of-Life Action".
Main flow:
1.	System requests a sustainable-action recommendation (UC-12) and shows it with reason and confidence.
2.	Representative selects an action and gives a reason.
3.	System sets the asset to Pending Disposition and notifies the sustainability officer.
4.	Officer approves.
5.	Representative records completion: recipient or vendor, date, certificate.
6.	System sets the final state and updates waste-diversion figures.
Alternative flows: 4a. Officer rejects or chooses reuse/repair instead: asset returns to its previous status, or is published (UC-4).
Exception flows: 4b. Asset flagged hazardous with no safety inspection: system blocks approval until one is recorded (NFR-6).
Postconditions: Final state set and read-only; decision and AI recommendation logged.
Rules: BR-5, BR-6, BR-8.
UC-12: Recommend Sustainable Action
↗ UC-12 use-case diagram
Description: The AI suggests the best next life-cycle step for an asset.
Actors: AI Service (secondary); started by UC-11 («include») or UC-10 when an asset is Not Repairable («extend»).
Preconditions: Asset condition, age, maintenance history and value are recorded.
Trigger: UC-11 step 1, or UC-10 exception 1b.
Main flow:
1.	System sends the asset's condition, age, repair history and value to the AI Service.
2.	AI Service returns one action (retain, transfer, repair, refurbish, donate, disassemble, recycle, dispose), a reason and a confidence value.
3.	System shows the recommendation to the authorised user.
4.	User accepts, changes or dismisses it; no state changes without approval (FR-AI-11).
Alternative flows: 2a. Confidence below 0.6: shown as "Low confidence – review carefully".
Exception flows: 1a. AI Service unavailable: user proceeds without a recommendation.
Postconditions: Recommendation and the user's decision logged.
UC-13: Ask AI Assistant
↗ UC-13 use-case diagram
Description: A user asks in plain language to search, request, check status or get policy guidance.
Actors: Any Campus User (primary); AI Service (secondary).
Preconditions: User is logged in.
Trigger: User types a question in the assistant panel.
Main flow:
1.	User asks, e.g. "Is there a working projector in Engineering?"
2.	System sends the question and the user's role to the AI Service.
3.	AI Service chooses an allowed system function (e.g. search).
4.	System runs the function with the user's permissions.
5.	Assistant replies with results and links to the records.
Alternative flows: 3a. User asks to submit a request: assistant fills a summary; user confirms; system runs UC-6.
Exception flows:
•	4a. Function not permitted for the role: system refuses and explains which role can do it.
•	2a. AI Service unavailable: assistant shows "Assistant unavailable – use Search" with a link to UC-5.
Postconditions: Conversation and any action logged; no data outside the user's permissions shown.
UC-14: Generate Sustainability Report
↗ UC-14 use-case diagram
Description: A monthly report of reuse, savings and environmental impact, with an AI-written summary.
Actors: Sustainability Officer (primary); Procurement/Finance Officer (secondary, reader); AI Service (secondary).
Preconditions: User has report permission; data exists for the chosen period.
Trigger: User selects a month and department and clicks "Generate Report".
Main flow:
1.	System computes totals: transfers, avoided purchases, savings (AED), waste diverted (kg), CO2e, reuse rate, maintenance costs.
2.	System sends the totals (not raw records) to the AI Service.
3.	AI Service returns a narrative summary and suggested actions.
4.	System checks that every number in the text matches the computed totals.
5.	System shows the report with tables, charts and the summary; user exports PDF or CSV.
Alternative flows: 3a. AI Service unavailable: system shows the tables and charts without the narrative.
Exception flows: 4a. A number in the text does not match: system removes the narrative and flags it for regeneration.
Postconditions: Report saved with date, period and model version.
Rules: FR-AI-17 (figures from the database only).
UC-15: Manage Users and Roles
↗ UC-15 use-case diagram
Description: The administrator controls who can use the system and what they can do.
Actors: System Administrator (primary).
Preconditions: Administrator is logged in.
Trigger: Administrator opens "User Management".
Main flow:
1.	Administrator creates or selects a user.
2.	Administrator sets name, email, department and roles.
3.	System validates and saves the account.
4.	Administrator may also add or edit departments, locations and asset categories (FR-ACC-5).
Alternative flows: 1a. Administrator deactivates an account: system confirms first, then blocks the user's next login.
Exception flows: 3a. Email already used: system refuses and shows the existing account.
Postconditions: Account and role changes saved and audited.
UC-16: Edit Asset
↗ UC-16 use-case diagram
Description: An authorised user corrects or updates an asset's details or status.
Actors: Department Representative or Asset Custodian (primary).
Preconditions: Asset belongs to the user's department; asset is not in a final state.
Trigger: User clicks "Edit" on the asset page.
Main flow:
1.	System shows the current values.
2.	User changes fields (e.g. location, condition, photos) or the status.
3.	System validates the fields and checks the status change is allowed (FR-AST-6).
4.	System saves the changes and keeps the old values in history (FR-AST-5).
Alternative flows: 2a. User cancels: nothing changes.
Exception flows: 3a. Status change not allowed (e.g. Disposed → Available): system refuses and explains. 3b. Invalid field: system highlights it.
Postconditions: Asset updated; edit event in history.
UC-17: Withdraw Listing
↗ UC-17 use-case diagram
Description: A department removes an asset from the marketplace or acts on an expired listing.
Actors: Department Representative or Asset Custodian (primary).
Preconditions: Asset is Available and has no Approved request.
Trigger: User clicks "Withdraw", or the listing reaches its end date (FR-PUB-4).
Main flow:
1.	System asks for a reason.
2.	User confirms.
3.	System removes the listing and sets the asset back to In Use.
4.	System cancels any queued requests and notifies those requesters.
Alternative flows: 1a. Listing expired: system asks the user to extend, withdraw, or propose a disposition (goes to UC-11).
Exception flows: 2a. Asset has an Approved request: system blocks the withdrawal (FR-PUB-3).
Postconditions: Listing removed; event logged.
UC-18: Track or Cancel Request
↗ UC-18 use-case diagram
Description: A requester follows their requests and can cancel one before approval.
Actors: Requester (primary).
Preconditions: Requester has at least one request.
Trigger: Requester opens "My Requests".
Main flow:
1.	System lists the requester's requests with state and dates (FR-REQ-4).
2.	Requester opens one to see its full history.
3.	Requester clicks "Cancel" on a Submitted or Under Review request.
4.	System sets it to Cancelled and releases the reservation (FR-REQ-5).
Alternative flows: 3a. Requester only views status: flow ends at step 2.
Exception flows: 3b. Request already Approved: cancel is not offered; requester is told to contact the department. 1a. Reservation passed 3 working days: system shows it as Expired (FR-REQ-6).
Postconditions: Request state updated; owning department notified of any cancellation.
UC-19: Report Defect
↗ UC-19 use-case diagram
Description: A custodian reports that an asset is faulty so maintenance can act.
Actors: Asset Custodian (primary); Maintenance Staff (secondary, notified).
Preconditions: Asset is in the custodian's care and not in a final state.
Trigger: Custodian clicks "Report Defect".
Main flow:
1.	Custodian describes the fault and adds an optional photo (FR-MNT-1).
2.	System creates a maintenance job in Reported state.
3.	System sets the asset to Under Maintenance and notifies maintenance staff (FR-MNT-2).
Alternative flows: 1a. Asset is currently listed: system pauses the listing until repaired.
Exception flows: 1b. Description empty: system asks for it before saving.
Postconditions: Maintenance job open; UC-10 can start.
UC-20: View Asset History
↗ UC-20 use-case diagram
Description: A user sees the complete life-cycle story of an asset.
Actors: Campus User (primary), limited by role.
Preconditions: User is logged in and may view the asset.
Trigger: User opens the "History" tab of an asset.
Main flow:
1.	System loads all recorded events for the asset (FR-AUD-1).
2.	System shows them as a timeline, newest first, with date, user and old/new values (FR-AUD-2).
3.	User filters by event type if needed.
Alternative flows: 2a. Requester role: cost and value fields are hidden.
Exception flows: 1a. User tries to edit or delete an entry: no such option exists; history is read-only (FR-AUD-3).
Postconditions: None (read-only).
UC-21: View Audit Log
↗ UC-21 use-case diagram
Description: The administrator checks who did what across the whole system, including AI decisions.
Actors: System Administrator (primary).
Preconditions: Administrator is logged in.
Trigger: Administrator opens "Audit Log".
Main flow:
1.	Administrator searches by user, asset, action and date range (FR-AUD-5).
2.	System shows matching entries, including AI recommendations with model version, confidence and final decision (FR-AUD-4).
3.	Administrator exports the results to CSV.
Alternative flows: 1a. No filter: system shows the latest 100 entries.
Exception flows: 2a. No match: system shows "No entries found". Entries can never be edited or deleted (FR-AUD-3).
Postconditions: None stored; the view itself is logged.
UC-22: Generate Operational Report
↗ UC-22 use-case diagram
Description: Standard reports on assets, requests, transfers, maintenance and approvals.
Actors: System Administrator (primary).
Preconditions: Administrator is logged in.
Trigger: Administrator opens "Reports" and picks a report type.
Main flow:
1.	Administrator selects the report (available assets, open requests, completed transfers, maintenance history, ownership changes, approvals or asset status).
2.	Administrator sets department and date range.
3.	System builds the report from the database (FR-RPT-1).
4.	Administrator exports it as PDF or CSV (FR-RPT-2).
Alternative flows: 2a. No filter: report covers all departments for the current month.
Exception flows: 3a. No data in range: system shows an empty report with a message.
Postconditions: Report generated; export logged.
