7.1 Prioritisation
The prototype must deliver every Must requirement; Should items follow if time allows; Could items are future work.
Priority	Scope	Examples
Must – essential prototype	Login and roles, asset registration, publishing, search, requests and reservations, approvals, transfers, history and audit, basic reports, the five required AI features with explanations, human approval and AI fallback, core sustainability figures	FR-ACC-1–3, FR-AST-1–6, FR-REQ-1–5, FR-APR-1–5, FR-TRF-1–4, FR-AI-1–7, 9–11, 13–18, FR-SUS-1–2, 6
Should – desirable extension	Account lockout, request queue, email copies, PDF/CSV export, sustainability ranking, waste and CO2e estimates, WCAG 2.2 AA	FR-ACC-4, FR-REQ-3, FR-NOT-2, FR-RPT-2, FR-AI-8, FR-SUS-3–5, NFR-16
Could – future enhancement	Listing expiry prompts, high-value second approval, missing-metadata prompts, Arabic assistant, fairness monitoring	FR-PUB-4, FR-APR-6, FR-AI-3, FR-AI-13 (Arabic), NFR-25
Won't (this release)	Public access, live integration with university ERP/procurement systems, demand forecasting	—
7.2 Classic vs AI-Enhanced Requirements
Need	Classic (deterministic) requirement	AI-enhanced requirement	Human control
Find an asset	FR-SRC-1–2 keyword and filters	FR-AI-4–7 semantic match with score and reason	Requester chooses
Describe an asset	FR-AST-1 category picked from list	FR-AI-1–2 suggested category and tags	User accepts or edits
End-of-life decision	FR-EOL-1 manual proposal	FR-AI-10–12 recommended action	Sustainability officer approves
